00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.960
Your attack surface just got a lot bigger.&nbsp;
How? Well, generative AI. This technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.520
that is taking the world by storm and can do&nbsp;
amazing stuff, also introduces some new threats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:19.400
with it and new risks. In fact, 4 out of 5&nbsp;
executives have said that they're not sure they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.240
can really trust generative AI because they have&nbsp;
concerns specifically related to cybersecurity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:30.880
They have concerns related to privacy and&nbsp;
accuracy of what the system can do. If we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.560
don't address those issues, we don't have trust&nbsp;
and we're not able to take full advantage of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:42.480
technology. So what do we need to do in order to&nbsp;
secure generative AI? Well, we need a framework&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.200
that will allow us to take a look at these issues.&nbsp;
So let's talk a little bit about what this is and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:55.160
how it works. Well, what starts is we have data.&nbsp;
We use this data from a lot of different sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:03.760
and we use it to train and tune our models, which&nbsp;
are the next component of this framework. We use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:11.200
the data to train the model and then ultimately&nbsp;
the model is used for inferencing. The inferencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.520
is where we get our output. So this is how the&nbsp;
thing generally works. In fact, we have to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.280
at how we're going to secure each one of those.&nbsp;
How do I secure the data? How do I secure the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.240
model? How do I secure the usage? And it turns&nbsp;
out there's a couple of other things we need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:33.160
take a look at that I'll show you before the video&nbsp;
is over. Okay, let's take a look at securing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:39.080
data itself. This is the data that we're going&nbsp;
to use to train and tune the model. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:44.560
a bad guy is going to look at that and see what&nbsp;
they can do to it because that's what they do. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.360
it exists, they're going to try to mess it up.&nbsp;
This is kind of why we can't have nice things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:55.760
I'll tell you. And what they're going to do in&nbsp;
this case, one possibility is poison the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;-

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:01.480
If they poison the data, then basically they&nbsp;
introduce some inaccuracies or things like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:08.280
that go then into the training and tuning of the&nbsp;
model. And now the model produces bad results as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:15.400
a consequence of that. Another thing that could&nbsp;
happen in this space is exfiltration. An exfil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:21.560
attack would be where someone breaks into this&nbsp;
system and pulls data away from it. And this could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:29.400
maintain a large set of sensitive information. And&nbsp;
another version of that is leakage. Leakage might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:35.800
be a more unintentional versus exfiltration is&nbsp;
an intentional where someone has come in. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:41.240
effect is the same. This sensitive data--and we may&nbsp;
use a lot of really sensitive data to train our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:47.960
model and tune it because that will make it more&nbsp;
accurate and more useful in our use case. But as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:54.760
result, now this ends up with a big bullseye on it&nbsp;
that somebody is going to try to hit. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:59.040
we're going to have to try to secure that. How&nbsp;
could we do that? Some things that we should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:06.960
be taking note of is we should do data discovery&nbsp;
and classification. I need to know where all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:12.680
sensitive data is. Maybe these data sources were&nbsp;
secured when they were in their various databases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.760
But now that we brought all this together as a&nbsp;
training system, maybe we didn't apply the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:24.600
controls and we need to. Classify that so that we&nbsp;
know what level of security and protections are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:32.880
necessary. We want to also use cryptography so&nbsp;
that we make sure that the data that does leak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:39.880
out or gets exfiltrated out is of no harm. No one&nbsp;
can read it, unless they're an authorized user. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:46.840
also add things like access controls, which make&nbsp;
sure that only authorized people can get into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:53.520
system using strong multifactor authentication&nbsp;
(MFA) and things like that. And then also we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:04:00.160
to monitor the system. I want to know if this&nbsp;
has occurred so that I can then take remedial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:06.800
actions and do the right sort of countermeasures&nbsp;
and limit the exposure. Okay, now let's talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.840
about securing the model itself. Now, how does&nbsp;
this stuff work? It turns out very few people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:18.400
develop models for their organization because it's&nbsp;
very computationally expensive, requires a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.320
effort. So most people are leveraging existing&nbsp;
models. In many cases, they get them from open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.280
source. And knowing where you're getting your&nbsp;
model from makes all the difference in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.800
So typically I would import models from a lot of&nbsp;
different trusted sources. But if I'm not careful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:42.040
maybe I get a model that I think is trusted&nbsp;
and it's not. Maybe it's a copy of a trusted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:48.440
one that has been modified in some way. And so&nbsp;
that means I really have to consider basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:54.320
the models as a supply chain, and I have to do&nbsp;
supply chain management of the models themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.760
that go into whatever system I'm going to use. So&nbsp;
in this case, I need to be able to make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:06.840
I can trust what my sources are, and I need to be&nbsp;
able to know that there are not bad things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:11.800
not just bad information, actual malware. There&nbsp;
have been researchers that have done a proof of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:17.240
concept to show that malware could be introduced&nbsp;
through a machine learning model. So we have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:22.680
be able to look for those kinds of things. Also,&nbsp;
in many cases, we're going to use APIs as a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:28.280
to communicate out to other services--maybe to&nbsp;
communicate with a model if we're not hosting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:35.560
it directly ourselves. That API path could also&nbsp;
be a path where an attacker could come in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:42.040
introduce some sort of error that then affects&nbsp;
our system. Other things--if we use plug-ins that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:48.160
elevate privilege, well, I need to be concerned&nbsp;
about the privilege escalation that can occur and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:55.000
limit what it's able to do so that it's not able&nbsp;
to also make changes here, or put bad information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:00.600
out, or even modify parts of my system. I want&nbsp;
to make sure that I have a human in the loop to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:06.240
guard against some of those kinds of things. And&nbsp;
then as well, I've been talking about IT attacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:12.200
but how about an IP attack, intellectual property?&nbsp;
I need to be able to make sure that I'm not using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:18.560
copyrighted works in one of these sources, because&nbsp;
then I could be sued for using information that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.120
not really I'm permitted to be using. So what&nbsp;
kinds of things should we be doing to guard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:29.160
against this? Well, I need to scan my systems&nbsp;
just like I do for other types of malware and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:35.880
look for any type of of of harmful code that's&nbsp;
being introduced into the system. I need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:42.200
able to harden my system. That basically means I'm&nbsp;
going to remove any unnecessary services or change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:47.520
all the default user ID and passwords. All of&nbsp;
those kinds of things. The system should be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:54.040
to withstand an attack. And I do that hardening by&nbsp;
removing and limiting the attack surface as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.040 --> 00:07:02.120
as I can. Then I use things like role-based access&nbsp;
control. Again, trying to make sure that something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:07.720
like this can't do more than it's supposed to&nbsp;
be able to do. And I can do that on a per user,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:14.080
per account basis. And then look at my&nbsp;
sources, vet those sources, make sure that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:19.240
are trustworthy, make sure that they don't contain&nbsp;
copyrighted materials that will get me in trouble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:27.360
legally and things like that. So while these are&nbsp;more IT, this is IP. 
Okay, we're going to take a look at how do we secure the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:33.280
usage of our generative API? We've looked at the&nbsp;
data, the model--how it's used. And it turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:39.080
out one of the main ways that this system can&nbsp;
be manipulated by a bad guy, a malicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:46.800
actor, is through what's called prompt injection.&nbsp;
And prompt injection is in fact the #1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:53.040
on a list of top 10 vulnerabilities mentioned by&nbsp;
OWASP, if you're familiar with that organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:58.200
So prompt injection. In that case, we have a user&nbsp;
that comes in and basically tries to jailbreak the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:03.840
large language model or generative model that's&nbsp;
behind this by issuing commands, having the system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:09.080
do things that it was not intended to do. Trying&nbsp;
to get around the guardrails that are put there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:14.960
It's almost a semantic attack. It's an attack with&nbsp;
the words on the knowledge itself in many cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:21.040
So we basically with that can also sometimes bias&nbsp;
the output and therefore the information that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:26.800
comes out of the system is now not as trustworthy&nbsp;
as it should be. Another type of attack someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:34.440
may do is a denial of service. If I send enough&nbsp;
attacks into the system that are complex, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:40.440
maybe it starts bogging the system down. Because&nbsp;
I can ask a question much faster than a system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:45.560
can answer it. So we send in a bunch of these and&nbsp;
now the system becomes overwhelmed and it can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:52.720
keep up anymore. And then the last one that I'll&nbsp;
mention here is model theft. In this case, someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:57.880
might not be able to just break in and steal your&nbsp;
model if you've done a good job up here in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:04.320
storing of your data and securing all of that.&nbsp;
But what if they just put a number of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:10.320
queries into the system and then take the output&nbsp;
back and do that again and again and again. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:15.800
can basically mine the model in order to get&nbsp;
the information that they want. Then they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:20.840
potentially go build their own version of this.&nbsp;
And now they've essentially stolen your model in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:26.800
the process. So what can we do to guard against&nbsp;
some of these threats? Well, one of the things we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:34.920
should do is monitor. In this case, monitor the&nbsp;
inputs into the system and put guardrails around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:40.480
those. We'll never be able to put all the kinds&nbsp;
of semantic guardrails that we need to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:47.040
that someone can never get in. But we need to at&nbsp;
least try and do the best job we can. There also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:52.120
will probably be a new class of tools that we're&nbsp;
starting to see emerge. One example of this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:58.000
machine learning, detection and response. So this&nbsp;
is we've done detection and response and security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:03.080
for a long time, but something that's specifically&nbsp;
built for machine learning, maybe even generative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:08.200
models. This is going to be a new emerging area&nbsp;
that we can start looking at. And then finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:14.480
some of our fundamentals using security&nbsp;
information, event management system, or a SOAR,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:20.520
a security orchestration, automation and response&nbsp;
system that is monitor all of this so that I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:27.680
see the abnormal inputs. I can see if the system&nbsp;
is under duress, if it's being overloaded, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:33.560
things like that. If someone's carrying data out.&nbsp;
I could use some of those type of capabilities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:39.711
order to be aware. So there you have a way that&nbsp;
you can secure the usage as well.

00:10:39.711 --> 00:10:46.880
Okay, now for the big reveal of the two elements I told you I&nbsp;
would talk about at the end. AI, generative AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:54.960
does not exist in a vacuum. It runs on IT systems.&nbsp;
Therefore, we have an infrastructure underneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:00.320
all of this--traditional computers. And the way&nbsp;
we do security for those is something that I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:06.280
talked about before. That's what supports all of&nbsp;
these systems and makes it possible for them to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:12.360
what they do. And what I've discussed before, is&nbsp;
this thing called the CIA Triad: confidentiality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:18.640
integrity and availability. Those are the concerns&nbsp;
that we have in securing an IT system. Those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:23.840
concerns don't go away just because now we move&nbsp;
to an AI system. We have to do everything we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:28.680
always done, plus a little bit more. And that&nbsp;
little bit more is all of these kinds of things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:34.240
that I've been talking about in the video. But&nbsp;
we can't ignore the fundamentals of securing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:40.160
infrastructure itself and doing the basic blocking&nbsp;
and tackling that we always have to do on every IT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:47.360
system. And then finally, the last element here is&nbsp;
one of governance. Not so much a security concern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:53.880
but it's a big concern for the functional&nbsp;
operation and correct results of the system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.880 --> 00:12:01.160
I need to be able to direct, manage, and monitor&nbsp;
how the generative AI works. I need to ensure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:06.760
it's fair, that it's not biased, that the model&nbsp;
doesn't have drift over time because someone has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:11.280
introduced some incorrect information in it.&nbsp;
I need to be able to ensure that that's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:16.800
occurring. I need to be able to keep up with&nbsp;
regulatory compliance and requirements issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:22.280
And ultimately make sure that the system operates&nbsp;
ethically. So I need a governance layer here as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:29.280
well. Put all of these things together and this&nbsp;
is a security framework for generative AI. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:34.440
if we think about--to summarize--what I've&nbsp;
talked about in another video, is using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:40.360
AI to create better security. And there are a&nbsp;
lot of possibilities of what generative AI can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:47.040
do in order to make us better. Make this a force&nbsp;
multiplier for doing the best cybersecurity we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:53.360
ever done. And then what has been the subject of&nbsp;
this video is how we can use security to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.360 --> 00:13:00.640
that the AI is secure. So it's AI for security&nbsp;
and security for AI. If we get all of that right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:05.520
then we win. Thanks for watching. If you found&nbsp;
